## Fonts vendored in this repository

Five families ship as `.ttf` under `assets/fonts/`, each with its full licence
text beside it. All five are under the SIL Open Font License, Version 1.1.

| Family | Copyright, as stated in its OFL.txt | Licence text |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Inter | Copyright 2020 The Inter Project Authors (https://github.com/rsms/inter) | `assets/fonts/inter/OFL.txt` |
| Atkinson Hyperlegible | Copyright 2020 Braille Institute of America, Inc. | `assets/fonts/atkinson/OFL.txt` |
| IBM Plex Mono | Copyright © 2017 IBM Corp. with Reserved Font Name "Plex" | `assets/fonts/plexmono/OFL.txt` |
| Poppins | Copyright 2020 The Poppins Project Authors (https://github.com/itfoundry/Poppins) | `assets/fonts/poppins/OFL.txt` |
| Space Grotesk | Copyright 2020 The Space Grotesk Project Authors (https://github.com/floriankarsten/space-grotesk) | `assets/fonts/spacegrotesk/OFL.txt` |

**Derived works.** `src/core/render/fonts/*.json` are monochrome glyph atlases
rasterised from those faces by `tools/gen_font_atlas.py`, and they are
committed so that rendering needs no font installed on the machine and no asset
off it. The OFL permits redistribution of the fonts and of derived works under
the same licence, which is why these files can live in git. The atlases are
named for the families they were rasterised from and are not distributed as
fonts under a reserved name; IBM Plex Mono's reserved name is "Plex", and
nothing here is offered as a font called Plex.

Two of these faces are also served to the browser as webfonts by
`next/font/local`: **Space Grotesk** sets the whole interface, and **IBM Plex
Mono** sets labels, timestamps and technical values. Space Grotesk ships as the
single variable file `SpaceGrotesk-Variable.ttf`, covering weights 300 to 700 —
the upstream project publishes no static SemiBold, and the interface uses 500
and 600. IBM Plex Mono ships as four static cuts (400, 500, 600, 700) for the
same reason.

Both are self-hosted from this repository at build time. Nothing is fetched
from a font CDN at runtime, and `tools/screenshots.ts` fails its QA run on any
request to an origin other than the tower's own, so this is checked rather than
intended.

Poppins is no longer served to the browser — Space Grotesk replaced it — but it
stays vendored, because `src/core/render/fonts/poppins.json` was rasterised
from it and a composition may still select that face for the panel.

**Not included, deliberately:** the earlier version of the atlas generator
rasterised macOS Arial, which is licensed to Apple by Monotype for use on the
machine and is not ours to redistribute as derived bitmaps. It was removed.

## Symbols

The pictograms in `src/core/render/pictograms.ts` are original 12×12 drawings
made for this project, in the panel's four pigments. No emoji font is used or
redistributed.

## Network services the running program can read

Four classes of outbound request exist, and they are listed here in full. All
four are optional, all four are read-only, none is contacted unless a dashboard
binds to a module that needs it, and none of them is on in a fresh install.

1. **Open-Meteo** (`api.open-meteo.com`), the forecast, when
   `NOTE4C_WEATHER_LATITUDE` and `NOTE4C_WEATHER_LONGITUDE` are set. Open-Meteo
   publishes its data under **CC BY 4.0**, which requires attribution. This
   product attributes it on the panel itself: the weather module can print the
   location and the source — "… · Open-Meteo" — beside the numbers.
2. **A second forecast source**, whatever URL is set in
   `NOTE4C_WEATHER_FALLBACK_URL`, used only when the first one fails. The
   parser expects the shape published by Environment and Climate Change
   Canada's city page feed, which is why this is a whole URL rather than a
   place. It is an arbitrary origin supplied by the operator, so this project
   asserts nothing about it: if you point it at a government or commercial
   service, that service's terms of use — including any attribution it
   requires — are yours to honour.
3. **A Home Assistant instance**, at `HASS_URL`, read with the long-lived token
   in `HASS_TOKEN` (or in the file named by `NOTE4C_HA_ENV_PATH`). This is the
   only one of the four that sends a credential. It is your own instance, under
   your own terms; the tower reads `sensor.*` and `binary_sensor.*` states and
   nothing else, and writes nothing.
4. **A second renderer**, at `NOTE4C_COMPOSER_ORIGIN` — another program that
   may be running on the same machine or LAN. The tower reads its status
   endpoint, never writes to it, never takes its scheduler lock and never calls
   its upload path. Unset by default, in which case it is not mentioned in the
   interface at all.

Separately, the tower talks to **the NOTE4C panel itself** on the LAN: an
RFC1918 IPv4 literal on port 80, over a closed path allowlist, with redirects
refused. That is the point of the program rather than an optional extra, and it
is documented under "Network behaviour" in the README.

## A process boundary, not a network one

**Apple Reminders.** When `NOTE4C_REMINDCTL_BIN` names one, the tower spawns
`remindctl` — a third-party command-line program, not part of this repository
and not redistributed by it. It is spawned with no shell, no stdin, a hard
timeout, and only the `list`, `show` and `status` subcommands; every mutating
subcommand is refused by an explicit list. That binary holds Full Access to
Reminders, which is access to the whole iCloud Reminders database; the narrowing
is done on this side, and what leaves the adapter is two counts and a list name.
Reminder `notes` are dropped at the parse boundary and are unreachable from any
code path here. Unset by default, in which case no process is spawned.

Beyond those four network classes, the device, and that one subprocess, nothing
else leaves the machine. The tower binds loopback, and serves no asset from a
third party.

## Dependencies

Runtime: `next`, `react`, `react-dom`, `zod` and `tsx`, all MIT. `tsx` is a
runtime dependency rather than a development one because `npm start` and
`npm run dev` go through `tools/tower-serve.ts`, the launcher that applies the
bind policy before the server exists. Development: `typescript` and
`@playwright/test` (Apache-2.0), `vitest` and the `@types/*` packages (MIT).

Those direct dependencies pull in a transitive tree that also contains
MPL-2.0 (`lightningcss`), LGPL-3.0-or-later (`@img/sharp-libvips-*`), ISC,
0BSD, BSD-3-Clause and CC-BY-4.0 (`caniuse-lite`) components, all of them
underneath Next.js. None of them is vendored here: npm installs them from the
registry under their own terms, and this repository redistributes no part of
them.

